<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        Besides pasting, the <a href="import">Import</a> page reads downloaded PDF statements and CSV exports, and pictures of a
        statement: a screen capture, a screenshot, or a photo from a webcam or your phone. Whatever you give it is read in your
        browser and turned into text in the <span class="help-ui">Pasted text</span> box, where you check it exactly as for a paste.
    </p>

    <h3>PDF statements and CSV exports</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Choose the account first.</li>
        <li>Drag the files onto <span class="help-ui">Drag statement files here</span>, or click it to browse. Several at once is fine, such as
            all twelve months of a year; they're joined into one list.</li>
        <li>Check the preview and import.</li>
    </ol>
    <ul>
        <li>Most banks' PDF statements work, including ones that are locked against editing but open without a password.</li>
        <li><strong>Can't be read:</strong> statements that need a password to open, a few with the strongest (AES-256) encryption,
            and scanned statements (pictures of paper inside a PDF). For those, use pictures instead.</li>
        <li>Several CSV files are joined under the first file's header row, so import one bank's exports at a time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Mortgage and home equity line statements</h3>
    <p>
        A mortgage or home equity line (HELOC) statement gives what you owe as labelled figures, such as
        <em>Closing Principal Balance</em>, rather than a balance column. The import page recognises these and shows a
        <span class="help-ui">Loan statement</span> box with each part of what's owed:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Each portion's balance.</strong> A TD Home Equity FlexLine has a revolving portion and one or more term (mortgage)
            portions; each is listed. A negative revolving balance is a credit in your favour and lowers the total.</li>
        <li><strong>Deferred interest.</strong> When a payment didn't cover all the interest, the statement shows the deferred interest
            owing. It isn't included in the principal balance, so it's listed on its own and added to what you owe.</li>
        <li>Untick a part you track in a separate account, such as a term portion kept as its own mortgage account.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Leave <span class="help-ui">Set account balance</span> ticked to make the account's balance the total as of the statement date.
        The statement's payments appear in the preview as money in, each with its principal and interest in the description, filed
        as a transfer: the payment's other side, in your chequing account, is where the spending shows. With no payments to import,
        the button reads <span class="help-ui">Set balance</span>.
    </p>
    <p>
        If the account already has a balance dated after the statement (for example, one you typed in when you added the account),
        it's kept, and the result says so. Choose <span class="help-ui">Use the statement's balance</span> there to replace it.
    </p>

    <h3>Pictures of a statement</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Capture screen</span>: open your bank's transactions in another window or tab first, then pick it when your
            browser asks what to share. One capture is taken; scroll and capture again for more.</li>
        <li><strong>Paste a screenshot</strong>: press <kbd>Win</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>, select the transactions, then
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> on the import page.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Use camera</span>: a webcam. Fill the frame with one page, flat and well lit, then <span class="help-ui">Take photo</span>.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Send from phone</span>: shows a QR code. Scan it with your phone's camera, take the photo there, and it
            arrives here. Each code works once.</li>
        <li><span class="help-ui">Choose pictures</span>: image files you already have (PNG, JPEG or WebP).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Add one picture per page or screenful. Each new picture is read, then the table in the box is rebuilt from all of
        them together. <span class="help-ui">Start over</span> forgets the pictures so far.
    </p>
    <p class="help-caution">
        Reading text from a picture can mistake a character, such as a 5 for a 6. Compare the amounts and the in/out totals with
        your statement before importing.
    </p>

    <h3>If it reads nothing useful</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Check the columns under the box: a misread heading row can turn amounts into descriptions.</li>
        <li>For pictures, a sharper, straighter, larger image helps: zoom the bank page in before capturing.</li>
        <li>Pasting the same page as text, when your bank allows it, is the most reliable route of all.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
